# Site blocks to add to a Caddy that ALREADY runs on the VPS and holds ports
# 80/443 (see docs/self-hosting.md, "Behind an existing reverse proxy").
# Do not run this repo's bundled `caddy` service next to it.
#
# Pick ONE of the two upstream styles, depending on how the existing Caddy runs.
#
# A) Caddy runs directly on the host (systemd): proxy to the loopback ports the
#    compose file publishes (127.0.0.1:3000 for web, 127.0.0.1:8000 for api).
#    Check first that nothing else already listens on those two ports:
#        ss -ltnp | grep -E ':(3000|8000)\b'
#
# B) Caddy runs in Docker: join its container to the `vigilo-self-host_default`
#    network and use the container names instead (second pair of lines below).
#
# After editing the existing Caddyfile:  caddy validate --config <path>  then reload
# (systemctl reload caddy, or `docker exec <caddy> caddy reload --config <path>`).
# Caddy issues the certificates for both names itself once their DNS points at
# this VPS, so create the A records first.

vigilo.onenexora.com {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
	# B) reverse_proxy vigilo-self-host-web-1:3000
}

vigilo-api.onenexora.com {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
	# B) reverse_proxy vigilo-self-host-api-1:8000
}
